 - in particular, split unitcombat_melee units into unitcombat_melee_polearm (i.e. using a lance (melee but from some distance), e.g. spearman, pikeman, etc if any more anyways etc) and unitcombat_melee_shock (i.e. close range / contact / brawl anyways etc, e.g. ancient maceman, axeman, swordsman, maceman, etc if any more anyways etc). For example, after this change, axemen now are still good against polearm units (they crush spears xd anyways etc) but they are now barely stronger than swords (just a bit as of now anyways etc); also, crossbows are still strong vs medieval macemen (unitcombat_melee_shock) but now useless vs pikes (unitcombat_melee_polearm anyways etc). This fixes the issue of bronze units like the axeman being stronger than medieval pikes which was non-sensical considering the age (in this case i mean but anyways etc) and cost difference anyways etc, or if not this one of any unit we'd want to add or rework. Also, some civ-specific variants like as of now the persia_immortal (swordsman based) are now correctly typed as melee_polearm since they are holding a lance, which means their modifier vs melee_shock makes them bit stronger than other swords (which makes sense since they have a lance and fight from a distance vs them anyways etc), while their overall kit remains good enough to withstand well enough axes as we intend too anyways etc. Also allows for more fine tuning for example with the as of now babylonian bowman, or the generic crossbow, now being strong specifically against melee_shock and not vs melee_polearm (in other words crossbows and such are useless against pikes (since they are armor as in heavy defensive generally units or/and use a metaphorical or real shield but anyways etc) as we intend anyways etc). General idea for mounted units: Spearman > Chariot (UNITCOMBAT_MOUNTED_MELEE); generic Horse/Camel Archer (UNITCOMBAT_MOUNTED_RANGED) > Spearman; generic Horse/Camel Knights (UNITCOMBAT_MOUNTED_MELEE) < Pikemen (UNITCOMBAT_MELEE_POLEARM); generic Horse/Camel Cuirassiers > Pikemen (UNITCOMBAT_MELEE_POLEARM). See sevopedia or/and xml for details anyways etc.
 - some units are now in another combat type, for example as of now the mechanized_infantry is now in armored units, not in gun units anymore.
 - This also changes warfare tactics and such for some other unit combat where i made such small or not modifications in this case but anyways etc anyways etc, for example mounted units are now split into mounted melee and mounted range units, so for example pikemen as of now are strong against mounted melee (like the horse knight) but not against the mounted ranged (such as the horse archer or cuirassier for example anyways etc). Also note that some civ-specific units are not in same combat type than their generic "parent" if i may say anyways etc, for example the ballista elephant is mounted ranged vs the war elephant being mounted melee if i may say anyways etc. I have tweaked a bit the advantages of most of the mounted melee units to buff them relatively a bit as they would be a bit weaker otherwise if i am not mistaken anyways etc as of now at least anyways etc, also there are other small or not differences anyways etc such as the defend order or such too if i am not mistaken. Also for example the anti tank infantry and the gunship would now be strong against mechanized infantry if i am not mistaken for example too as of now at least it would be so if i am not mistaken anyways etc. The airship is now a recon unit, not an air unit anymore, meant to be a defensive more effective recon unit than the explorer (also note: the airship as of now cannot see submarines anymore unlike later recon unit(s), see sevopedia unit chart and sevopedia unit of the corresponding units for details), see sevopedia unit for updated values anyways etc.
-- new camel units related to the BONUS_CAMEL changes, as an alternative mount to the horse. Also reworked the arabian empire's civ-specific unit as well
 - All units now are affected by city defenses (walls, castles, etc if any more/other), even gunpowder and later on units, seems more realistic to me this way, as walls can help against guns and against all (almost?) units, actually even if not having the tech for guns itself. See also for details: bIgnoreBuildingDefense in CIV4UnitInfos.xml or/and (tranlslate to english ((following) web page (anyways etc)) using web browser or such) [https://gforestshade.github.io/kujira/post/civ4unitinfos/#bignorebuildingdefense](https://gforestshade.github.io/kujira/post/civ4unitinfos/#bignorebuildingdefense) for details (too anyways etc(.)) anyways etc...
 - fixed the stealth bomber and other units having a lower collateral damage limit than its base collateral damage (`<iCollateralDamage>100</iCollateralDamage>` vs `<iCollateralDamageLimit>50</iCollateralDamageLimit>`), which i don't know a lot about but seems like a mistake or inconsistent with most units (why put a higher than the limit damage, again i could be mistaken about how this works-functions but anyways etc, but we (i.e. me but anyways etc xd if i may say but anyways etc...) also wanted to add more collateral damage support so now all units have some (swordsmen could throw shurikens) collateral damage limit, even though collateral damage itself starts at 0 for most units still; such info is now displayed in sevopedia unit chart (as of now named "Combat" category, as well as in each unit's sevopedia unit placeSpecial (as of now named "Special Abilities" panel)), and also ingame in map view when hovering over the unit, in its effects panel (we also now show more reliably the extra collateral damage info there as well if i am not mistaken but anyways etc); also as a result and in line/following this change but anyways etc, as of now collateral damage 1 and 2 promotions are available to all eligible units (which should be all or almost all, see sevopedia promotion or sevopedia unit for details); hopefully i am not mistaken in doing so or/and it is helpful or not or yes or etc but anyways etc)
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5157+) Upgrade costs rework: AIs no longer have cheaper upgrade costs than human players (increased `iAIUnitUpgradePercent` from 50 to 100 (see xml for updated values anyways etc.)). Additionally, reduced upgrade costs for both human and AI players, in `CvUnit::upgradePrice`, to as of now 75% (tunable). So unlike in base advciv where AI paid half cost (too easily upgrade / too cheap but anyways etc.) and humans payed full cost (which btw felt too expensive ingame to me at least but anyways etc.), now both human and AI players pay the same upgrade cost, which is as of now 75% of what humans used to pay for unit upgrades.
+- for simplicity most unit upgrades are fairly straightforward and relaxed, for example all mounted units can upgrade to the next mounted tier without much restriction, for example chariots upgrade to horse_archers now not to knights anymore, war elephants upgrade to knights not to cuirassiers anymore, a horse archer can become a camel knight or a horse knight, and a camel archer can become a horse knight or a camel knight as well. I assume that the riders themselves can transition to another mount type so it would make sense to reinvest their skills in new mounts hence them being upgradeable if i am not mistaken anyways etc. In particular, longbows and crossbows, as well as macemen, and pikemen, now upgrade to musketmen not to riflemen anymore, which makes a lot of sense and is historically more accurate, as well as a lot more balanced as cuirassiers and such other gun units can't abuse these early ranged or melee units that would have no problem and be just as suited if not more anyways etc to have a gun as well, should help mostly AIs as they tend to build many longbowmen and macemen and such but perhaps also human players depending on strategy. Cuirassiers are a bit stronger (even though quite a bit more expensive too anyways etc) to compensate and rebalance them. Also note that as of now for similar reasons, dragoon units have been reworked, and now in particular anyways etc upgrade to tanks. There are also other unit upgrade and such related changes to other units as of now too anyways etc, see sevopedia ingame or/and tech tree view or/and xml for accurate/updated value(s) anyways etc
+- similarly, also changed/reworked upgrade paths for naval units if i may say anyways etc, and while doing so in particular found the very inconsistent or/and nonsensical ironclad not being able to "walk" on ocean tiles??????? Wtf if i may say but anyways etc.. So disabled this and now ironclad can "walk" on ocean tiles as all naval units of its era or around it if i am not mistaken, i didn't check if accurate but would be surprised if not, buffed ironclad too so it is now an upgrade to frigates for example anyways etc, see [README.md#military-tree-and-changes](/README.md#military-tree-and-changes) for details or/and for all upgrade changes, or/and sevopedia entries of the units or/and related sevopedia entries, or/and xml or docs in advciv-sas mod folder hopefully helpful and most importantly more balanced this way and making sense if i may say but anyways etc anyways etc anyways etc
+- new camel units related to the BONUS_CAMEL changes, as an alternative mount to the horse. Also reworked the arabian empire's civ-specific unit as well
+- relaxed some bonus requirements as well when relevant and not breaking balance too much or strategic value of the bonuses. For example, the japanese samurai can now also be built with copper, not only iron, as the macemen are. Without this the civ-specific unit would be weaker than the generic one which doesn't make sense, especially considering we can't build the generic one as a fallback if we are japan, even if we have copper. Or civ-specific variants of the swordsman class, now also use copper as an alternative bonus prereq: the gallic_warrior was, so no reason the roman_legionary or as of now the incan_quechua_warrior can't as well (note: as of now the generic swordsman still requires iron as the only posssible bonus, to keep the strategic importance of iron at that era / stage of the game anyways etc); similarly also for civ-specific variants of the crossbowman class (UNIT_CHINA_ZHUGE_NU and UNIT_KINGDOM_OF_BENIN_DISCIPLINED_CROSSBOWMAN) as it is relevant enough (according to chatgpt 5 and as i asked it as well i mean for the benin bronzes but anyways etc: "Bronze/copper hardware was common (Benin bronzes; Chinese bronze/iron mix long before ubiquitous steel)") and they were a bit too weak otherwise so hopefully helps buff them as well but anyways etc. The Holy Roman Houfnice can as well for example (historically plausible bronze artillery). Plus, historically for all these units, not nonsensical to value copper/bronze as a main or an alternative material for weapons from what i saw about iron vs copper information in a quick search if i may say in this case but anyways etc. Not overdoing it either as i want bonuses to otherwise stay very valuable and critical strategically, but not in a painful or maybe rather non-sensical way in this case i mean anyways etc.
 - some units are buildable at different times/tech, also for historical accuracy, see tech tree i.e. tech advisor for details anyways etc
 - rebalanced the warrior / ancient maceman in particular so it gives more base strength but less city combat, as well as now having animal combat modifier as well, and slightly higher cost.
 - barracks in particular now require as of now tech_hunting. Hopefully this helps avoiding a military beeline by AIs or human players, or selectively boosts military leaders more with this extra chance in the early game (for those that start with as of now tech_hunting) anyways etc.
